GhostWire Wi-Fi — bundled aircrack-ng suite (static aarch64)
============================================================

The Wi-Fi tab runs these as root from the app's own files dir (WifiTools.kt).
They are statically-linked aarch64 Android executables — self-contained, no
shared libraries, no Kali chroot / NetHunter needed. Drop them in assets/wifi/bin
before building a release; the app extracts and chmod's them on first use.

Layout the app expects:

  assets/wifi/bin/            (chmod 755 at runtime)
    airodump-ng   (core)      scan + CSV + pcap capture
    aireplay-ng   (core)      deauth / injection
    aircrack-ng   (core)      key recovery
    iw            (core)      interface enumeration / mode switch
    hcxdumptool   (phase 5)   PMKID capture
    hcxpcapngtool (phase 5)   handshake/PMKID hash export + confirm
    reaver, wash  (phase 5)   WPS pixie / PIN
    bully         (phase 5)   WPS fallback
    pixiewps      (phase 5)   WPS pixie-dust offline

Provenance / build
------------------
Built static for aarch64 Android (bionic, API 21) with the Android NDK using
kimocoder's cross-compile build system:
  https://github.com/kimocoder/kali-nethunter-tools   (GPL-3.0)
    ./docker-build.sh build-image
    ./docker-build.sh build --arch arm64 aircrack-ng iw reaver pixiewps hcxdumptool hcxtools
  binaries land in out/aarch64-linux-android/<tool>/bin/

Versions: aircrack-ng 1.7, iw 6.17, reaver 1.6.6 (t6x), hcxdumptool 7.0.1,
hcxtools 7.0.1, pixiewps 1.4.2, wash 1.6.6 (t6x), bully 1.4-00.

wash is the SAME binary as reaver (t6x builds wash as a symlink to reaver;
main.c dispatches on argv[0] basename), so it is shipped under the name "wash".
kimocoder's build-reaver.sh only copies reaver out (cp reaver || cp wash), so
wash is taken from the build tree and given the same TLS-alignment fix + strip.

bully (github.com/aanarchyy/bully, 1.4-00) has no kimocoder recipe. Built with
the same NDK (aarch64-linux-android21-clang) against the libpcap/libnl3 this
image builds for reaver:
  CC -O2 -fno-emulated-tls -D_GNU_SOURCE -I. -Iutils -Itls -Iwps -Icrypto \
     -Icommon -DUSE_INTERNAL_CRYPTO -DCONFIG_NO_RANDOM_POOL \
     -I<prefix>/libpcap/include -o bully bully.c -static \
     -L<prefix>/libpcap/lib -L<prefix>/libnl3/lib -lpcap -lnl-genl-3 -lnl-3
  then fix-tls-alignment.py bully 64; llvm-strip bully.
One source fix: bully.h's global array `times[]` collides with bionic's
times() at link ("duplicate symbol: times") — renamed `times[`->`pkt_times[`
in bully.h, 80211.c, bully.c.

NOTE (OP9 internal qcacld monitor): like reaver, wash and bully do not work
usefully on the internal radio (con_mode monitor delivers frames with a bad-FCS
radiotap flag that the reaver/bully 802.11 stack drops; wash fixed-channel mode
errors pcap_activate -9). airodump-ng works there, these do not. Both are for
an external OTG adapter (iw-based monitor); see WifiTools.

